B.6
Introduction to subordinated stochastic processes
Rather than indexing discrete stochastic processes with integers Xp0q, Xp1q, .., Xt, Xt 1, the process can be indexed
by a set of numbers t1, t2, ... where these numbers are themselves a realisation of a stochastic process with positive
increments, so that t1 ¤ t2 ¤ ... (see Bochner [1960], Feller [1971]). Hence, if Tptq is a positive stochastic process,
then a new process XpTptqq may be formed which is said to be subordinated to Xptq. Tptq is called the directed
process, and the distribution of ∆XpTptqq is said to be subordinate to the distribution of ∆Xptq. The following
theorem holds for very general classes of subordinated stochastic process with independent increments. It provides a
simple formula for calculating the variance of the increments, and shows that the variance is ﬁnite for processes having
increments with ﬁnite variance.
Theorem B.6.1 Let Xptq and Tptq be processes with stationary independent increments. Let the increments of Xptq
be drawn from a distribution with mean 0 and ﬁnite variance σ2. Let the increments of Tptq be drawn from a positive
distribution with mean α, independent of the increments of Xptq. Then, the subordinated stochastic process XpTptqq
has stationary independent increments with mean 0 and variance ασ2.
It says that if the directing process has a ﬁnite mean, then ∆XpTq will have a ﬁnite variance unless ∆T does not.
