C.2.2.2
Linear generators
The Linear Congruential Generator (LCG) is one of the best known PRNG where k  1, m prime, and it is deﬁned
by the recurrence relation
xn  paxn1   cq
mod m
where un  xn
m is the sequence of pseudorandom values at the nth step with 0   a   m the multiplier, 0 ¤ c   m the
increment and m ¡ 0 is the modulus. The most efﬁcient LCG have an m equal to a power of 2, most often m  232
or m  264 as it allows for the modulus operation to be computed by merly truncating the rightmost 32 or 64 bits.
LCGs are fast and require minimal memory (32 or 64 bits) to retain state. If higher quality random numbers are needed
and sufﬁcient memory is available ( 2 kilobytes) then the Mersenne Twister algorithm is a preferred choice. The
