C.3.5.2
XOR and Gray code
As shown in Table (C.2), XOR is only true when either of the two bits is true, else it is false. Note, if v1  0.1 then
v1 ¡¡ 3  0.0001. That is, v1 is right-shifted by 3 bits. The (binary-reﬂected) Gray code of an integer i is deﬁned as
graypiq  i ` r i
2s  p..b3b2b1q2 ` p..b4b3b2q
where the last bit is removed in the second binary representation. For example, given the decimal
• i  1  p001q2
then we get the Gray code
grayp1q  p001q2 ` p000q2  p001q2  1
Similarly, given the decimal
• i  2  p010q2
then we get
grayp2q  p010q2 ` p001q2  p011q2  3
Again, given the decimal
• i  3  p011q2
then we get
grayp3q  p011q2 ` p001q2  p010q2  2
If we carry on, we get the Gray code in Table (C.3). We see that the Gray code is simply a reordering of the nonnegative
integers within every block of 2m numbers from m  0, 1, ... We let cn be the index of the ﬁrst 0 digit from the right
in the binary representation of n  pb3b2b1q2. Then we get c0  1, c1  2, c2  1, c3  3, c4  1, c5  2 etc..
