D.4
State space models
A state space model is deﬁned by a measurement equation postulating the relationship between an observable vector
and a state vector, and a transition equation describing the generating process of the state variables. State space models
are an alternative formulation of time series with a number of advantages for forecasting.
1. All ARMA models can be written as state space models.
2. Nonstationary models (e.g., ARMA with time varying coefﬁcients) are also state space models.
3. Multivariate time series can be handled more easily.
4. State space models are consistent with Bayesian methods.
In general, the model consists of
Xt  FtSt   vt observed data
(D.4.12)
St  GtSt1   wt unobserved state
vt  Np0, Vtq observation noise
wt  Np0, Wtq state noise
where Xt is a pn, 1q vector of time series, and the state vector St is made of pm, 1q state variables. Further, vt, wt are
independent and Ft, Gt are known matrices, often time dependent because of seasonality, of dimension pn, mq and
pm, mq.
