This equation is the equilibrium solution for an economy with complete market, in which consumption is exogenous and prices are deﬁned by the ﬁrst order condition for the optimum. In a multiperiod model with complete
markets and state independent, intertemporally additive separable utility, there is a unique representative agent
utility function satisfying the above optimum condition. The kernel is the agent’s marginal rate of substitution (MRS)
as a function of aggregate consumption. The marginal rate of substitution (MRS) across time for an agent with an
intertemporally additively separable utility function is a function of the ﬁnal state and depend only on the current
state as a normalisation. Since the pricing kernel only depends on the MRS between future and current consumption,
this path independence is a key element in the recovery theorem.
