E.6.2
Introducing the recovery theorem
In a recent article, Ross [2013] assumed that the state-price transition function, ppXi, Xjq, was observable, and
solved the system in Equation (E.6.18) to recover the three unknowns: p1q the natural probability transition function
fpXi, Xjq, p2q the pricing kernel ξpXi, Xjq, and p3q the discount rate δ. Note, the notion of transition independence
(see Deﬁnition (E.6.1)) was necessary to separately determine the kernel and the natural probability distribution.
Without this restriction, we don’t have enough information to identify separately p1q and p2q just from the stateprice transition function. This is because in the Equation (E.6.16) there are more unknowns than equations. Other
approaches used the historical distribution of returns to estimate the unknown kernel and thereby link the historical
estimate of the natural distribution to the risk-neutral distribution (see Jackwerth et al.
[1996], Ait Sahalia et al.
[1998] [2000]). Alternatively, one can assume a functional form for the kernel. In an ideal world, Ross [2013]
showed that the equilibrium system in Equation (E.6.18) could be solved without the need of historical data or any
further assumptions than a transition independent kernel.
