E.6.3
Using implied volatilities
We assume that the market is complete along dimensions related to the underlying process. We also assume that
we observe a continuum of option prices in the state space domain. Implied volatilities are a function of the riskneutral probabilities, which is expressed as the product of the natural probabilities and the pricing kernel (risk aversion
and time discounting) (see Equation (E.6.15)). As such, they embody all of the information needed to determine
state prices. From the volatility surface and the formula for the value of a call option, one can derive the state-price
