F.5
Introduction to indifference pricing theory
F.5.1
Martingale measures and state-price densities
We saw in Appendix (F.1) that option pricing in complete markets consists in ﬁxing a measure Q under which the
discounted traded assets are martingales, and to calculate option prices via expectation under this measure. This is
related to the notion of a state-price-density from economics. The advantage of using a state-price-density ξT is that
prices π can be calculated as expectations under the physical measure, that is, π  ErξT CT s. In an incomplete
market there is more than one martingale measure, or equivalently, there are inﬁnitely many state-price-densities. For
example, we consider a model on a stochastic basis pΩ, pFtq0¤t¤T , Pq with a single traded asset with price process
St and a second auxiliary process Yt, which may correspond to a related but non-traded stock. The dynamics of the
