Note,the proposed price is for an individual with particular risk preferences (see Detemple et al. [1999]). In contrast
to the Black and Scholes price, utility indifference prices are non-linear in the number k of options. The investor is not
willing to pay twice as much for twice as many options, but requires a reduction in this price to take on the additional
risk. If the market is complete or if the claim CT is replicable, the utility indifference price πpkq is equivalent to the
complete market price for k units.
