Remark G.1.1 When the function hptq goes endlessly from 8   t   8, then its total power and power spectral
density will, in general, be inﬁnite. Of interest is then the power spectral density per unit time, which is computed by
taking a long, but ﬁnite, stretch of the function hptq, and then dividing the resulting PSD by the length of the stretch
used.
Parseval’s theorem in this case states that the integral of the one-sided PSD per-unit-time, which is a function of
frequency f, converges as one evaluates it using longer and longer stretches of data. To conclude, we are rarely given
a continuous function hptq to work with, but rather a list of measurements of hptiq for a discrete set of ti.
