G.4.2.3
The ϵ-decimated DWT
The DWT being an orthogonal transform, it corresponds to a particular choice of basis for the space RN where the
original sequence lies. As a result, depending on the choice of the basis, we can get modiﬁcations of the DWT.
We could also select every odd number of each sequence with the operator pD1xqj  x2j 1, getting the mapping
pD1Gx, D1Hxq which is an orthogonal transformation. Reconstruction would be obtained by successive application
of the corresponding inverse operator, R1. Further, if we let ϵJ1, .., ϵ0 be a sequence of 0 and 1, we can then use the
operator Dϵj at level j, and perform the reconstruction by using the corresponding sequence of operators Rϵj, giving a
different orthogonal transformation from the original sequence for each choice of the sequence ϵ. This transformation
is called the ϵ-decimated DWT. To understand the mechanism, we consider the shift operator S deﬁned by
pSxqj  xj 1
such that D1  D0S and, thus, R1S1R0. We can also see that SD0  D0S2 and that the operator S commutes
